# 15. CORE ML DEVELOPMENT

Build the disease model BEFORE trying to integrate every other feature.

Recommended approach:

1. Inspect the provided dataset
2. Understand classes
3. Create honest train/validation split
4. Preprocess images
5. Use transfer learning
6. Train baseline
7. Evaluate validation performance
8. Improve generalization
9. Evaluate correctly
10. Build inference function
11. Save model weights
12. Connect inference to backend
13. Test with new images

Potential architectures may include an appropriate pretrained CNN or ViT backbone.

Choose based on:

* Accuracy/generalization
* Training resources
* Inference speed
* Ease of deployment

Do not choose an unnecessarily huge model just because it sounds advanced.

---

# 16. DATA LEAKAGE RULE

Never train on the organizers' held-out judging dataset.

The core challenge evaluates the model on an unseen field-condition test set.

Do not use the held-out test set for:

* Training
* Fine-tuning
* Hyperparameter selection
* Manual optimization based on labels

Document the split honestly.

---

# 17. MODEL EVALUATION

For the core disease detector, report:

* Macro-F1
* Confusion matrix
* Per-class precision
* Per-class recall
* Accuracy as supplementary information
* Baseline comparison
* Failure cases

Macro-F1 is the primary metric.

Do not optimize only for raw accuracy.

The project should prioritize field-image generalization.

